Hello!

I asked how to conveniently change a record size of a binary VMS file
(the files contain 320-byte long records of VAX floating numbers, and
they were edited on a UNIX system and copied back with wrong record
size (512)). The editing process is asynchronous, and it is not
practical to initiate the copy from the VMS side---if it weren't so,
the task would have been easy, as there is a VMS utility that allows
one to copy data over the network and set the RMS structure of a resulting
file (EXCHANGE/NETWORK file/FDL=fdldescription).

Several people wrote me in response to my query (my thanks go to all
of them, listed later in this message). Some recommended creating a
template file with correct record lenght and using COPY/OVERLAY.
However, this works only when both original and new file have record
lengths that are a multiply of 512 bytes, which is not the case here.

Another idea was to use the PD FILE utility; it did not work for me,
because my version of FILE was compiled using GCC, and could only get
its parameters via a CLD mechanism; however, I had to call it as a
foreign command using lib$spawn().

Yet another idea that occurred to me was to use the EXCHANGE/NETWORK
utility for a _local_ file:
	lib$spawn(EXCHANGE/NETWORK OLDFILE NEWFILE/FDL=FIXED320) This
requires a use of an extra file (FIXED320.FDL) containing the PDL
description of a desired file format. Carl J Lydick
<carl@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU> suggested a way of sending this
descripiton directly to EXCHANGE via a mailbox; this, however, is
beyond the scope of this article.

Finally, dano@petrucio.ae.utexas.edu (Dano Carroll) sent me a Fortran
program that I enclose in part at the end that contains code to directly
manipulate the record length using the sys$modify call.


