References: <1992Sep19.234610.26621@decuac.dec.com> <1992Sep21.151623.28853@panix.com> <1992Sep21.165552.3354@memstvx1.memst.edu> <1992Sep22.001458.25025@decuac.dec.com> <1992Sep22.105246.3355@memstvx1.memst.edu>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 1992 00:37:35 GMT


In article <1992Sep22.105246.3355@memstvx1.memst.edu>, flowers@memstvx1.memst.edu (Harry Flowers) writes:
>
>Also, what are the differences between the DSSI and SCSI buses?  I've heard
>that they're similar, but what are the practical performance differences?
>Do they both scale up linearly as drives are added?  Since the physical
>drives are the same, why does the DSSI controller on the drive cost more?
>--
The principal difference between DSSI and SCSI drives is the intelligence of
the controller on the disk. SCSI controllers are fairly simple-minded -- they
can do seeks and buffered reads and writes, and (sometimes) bad block
replacement. The new SCSI standards allow a lot more intelligence, but they
weren't around when DSSI was defined.

DSSI disk controllers are essentially a miniature HSC controller. You can set
host to the drive and look at lots of interesting information such as seek
distance and transfer size histograms, current threads running etc. The
controller does local request re-ordering, read ahead, and other performance
optimizations. It implements the MSCP protocol, and tries to do the right
thing in logging errors.

The other improvements over SCSI are mainly in the area of signal integrity.
The original SCSI bus has lousy electrical characteristcs. (Digital engineers
should NOT design analog circuits.)

The new SCSI drives under development that implement the new SCSI standards
fix most of the problems with the original SCSI spec, and offer much higher
performance (e.g. 20 MB/sec for FWD SCSI) and signal integrity.

All that aside, the original question asked about the benefits of using DSSI
on a DECstation 5500. There is one very good reason -- if you need more than
7 devices, then DSSI disks on the integral controller will give *much* better
performance than SCSI disks on a KZQSA.
--
