Well, just how can DEC ensure their success?  I have my own opinions.  I tend
to ramble so if I lose you, don't worry (be happy).  

	1)  Double their amount of advertising 1st quarter 93
		
I believe that DEC has a rare opportunity to blunt their competition.  Sun and
Intel were both delayed with their latest systems/chips (SuperSparc has had
production problems and I'm not sure of the reason Intel delayed P5).  Perhaps
some of their eagerness to get something out the door is that they didn't want
to bump heads with Alpha.  I realize this isn't the whole story concerning the
delays so be lenient on me.  By doubling advertising and doing some heads up
comparisons, maybe DEC can garnish more of the "fence-sitters" that were
considering Intel/Sun.  If you're ever going to grab someones attention, you
have to turn their head in your direction.  The machine Charlie Matco talks
about in the latest Digital N/R is code-named  Triumph and reportedly WILL
sell for under $3000 (probably $2999 ;^).  Triumph needs to be plastered like 
that Chevy Geo commercial everyone enjoys.

	2)  Change their VMS upgrade policy

Tried and true arguments from a Uni* perspective run along these lines:

        "Yeah but your upgrades make your system cost prohibitive"

How can IBM afford to "give away" RS/6000 upgrades?  I don't know, but it makes
their systems more than a little attractive.  I applaud DEC's new licensing
(300 per user) but it may not be enough.  I think they really have to make it
affordable (read: comparable to RS/6000 upgrade policies for instance) for the
small shop/business to be able to go from 5.2 to 5.5.

	3)  Come out with a killer server

This machine is code-named Cobra.  The 7000 (code-named Laser) is really a
main-frame in sheep's clothing.  The Cobra (4000 class) HAS to be able to take
on Sun's server or DEC is going to be a seller of Alpha PC's.  I am watching
Sun take over a site and the 6000 class machines are hitting the recycling
bins.  DEC KNOWS that Sun's (IBM & HP also) price for their server is killing 
them.  Cobra better be at or below Sun-HP-IBM.   $250,000 just ain't cutting it 
anymore. 

Repeat after me:
		Bottom Line Bottom Line Bottom Line . . . 

It is my hope that we see a much more aggressive DEC now that Robert Palmer has
taken over.  Ken Olsen was wise in turning the company over to someone that is
"young and aggressive".  From reading I understand that Digital has never  had
commissions for their sales staff.  I believe the prevailing opinion is that
Mr. Palmer is going to change that (or maybe he already has?).  Sales seem to 
rise when based on a reward/commission basis.  Feel free to follow up and
correct where my ideas are unrealistic/wrong or to add your own.

				Rob

