Not me.  You might try the collection of disktab entries
on gatekeeper.dec.com.  They're in /pub/DEC/ultrix-disktabs.

>And a few more questions about formatting disks:
>2. Does ULTRIX 4.2a support a disk of this size( >2Meg )?

Strictly speaking ULTRIX doesn't Support any 3rd party disks.  Support
in my use means, tested and qualified, if something goes wrong, you can
call the CSC for help.

On all the existing versions of ULTRIX, the useful device size is
limited to 2 GB.  In the case of disks you can probably partition
it that you have a 2 GB partition and whatever is left over.  You
might be able to use the SCSI CAM driver to do direct SCSI commands
that won't have this limit, but that makes for a rather unportable
program.

>3. When installing a 3rd party disk, and chpt -q /dev/rrz2a returns:
>	No partition table found in superblock...
>	using default table from device driver.
>	....partition info.........
>  where does the driver get the Current partition table from?

For SCSI disks it comes from the sz_rzxx_sizes[] entry in /sys/data/scsi_data.c.

>
>4. [ Geometry related stuff deleted because I don't know the answer. ]

You might also try:

1.  DEVGETGEOM to see what ULTRIX things the geometry is.  If it can
    figure it out.

2.  Create a file system on the A partition.  This will put the
    default partition table on the disk.  Repartition so that you
    can access more than the entire disk is supposed to be.  For
    example:

	Partition D - 0 to (2^21 - 2)
	Partition E - another 2 GB.

    Then use something like dd(1) to read every block of the
    partition.  A reasonable device driver should give you an
    error when it reaches the end of the disk.  I tried this
    using the non-CAM SCSI driver on a V4.2 it worked as expected
    (on a RZ23, that had a "400,000" block C partition).
