>Harry Flowers                           Internet: FLOWERS@MEMSTVX1.MEMST.EDU
>Memphis State University                & Bitnet: FLOWERS@MEMSTVX1

DSSI is what I refer to as "DEC's Stupid SCSI Interface."  Of course, that's
my opinion, and mine alone.  DSSI is just an arbitrary, proprietary bus
design for connecting peripherals, that happens to support pass-through
command modes so that you can do cluster communications across it.

SCSI also supports a pass-through command mode, but its never used for
anything that I've ever seen.  However, you can take two workstations, change
one of their SCSI controller IDs so its not the same as the other, connect
them together via SCSI, and in theory, you should be able to transfer data
between the two at about 5Mb/s.  Of course, that doesn't compare to simply
connecting their ethernets together, but you get the point.

Personally, and again, this is just my opinion, I wouldn't buy a DSSI disk,
even with the marginal performance gain you may see with it.  Especially for
use in an environment where you may plan on upgrading machines faster than you
upgrade disks.  SCSI is not only an open technology (ever go to a SCSI non-
disclosure?), but you immediately have an offering of SCSI devices that DSSI
can only dream about (printers, image processors, scanners, even MIDI inter-
faces).

