Here in the states, this educational software distribution is split into
two programs - the CSLG, which provides licenses, and the Educational
Software Library, which provides media, docs and consulting.

The terms are currently being negotiated here at Michigan, and I'm
directly involved with the ESL and not the CSLG, so I may not get
the numbers quite right, but....


In past years, the CSLG has cost the University of Michigan roughly
$10K per year.  This has been in effect for at least three years, and
should continue next year.  (Again, I don't remember the exact number,
but it's definitely NOT $1 per license).

In the past, the ESL cost about $15,000 plus a per-machine fee between
$25 (for VAXstations) and several thousand dollars (e.g. for Physics'
6260 system).

Any DEC machine on campus could register in the CSLG.  Internally we
were only charged a $10 processing fee - the annual lump sum was
absorbed by the colleges and wasn't prorated to the users.

ESL membership, however, cost from $225 to $3000 per machine, as the
cost was prorated.  Furthermore, the ESL agreement explicitly
prohibited the copying of software from an ESL system to a non-ESL
one, even though the non-ESL machine had a valid license through CSLG.


For the coming year, the program has been changed.  There will be no
per-machine fee, and the lump-sum fee is now tied to the number of
machines:

	1 - 25 systems		$13,920
	26 - 100 systems	$20,400
	101 + systems		$30,321

