Message-ID: <1992Sep21.235403.24693@decuac.dec.com>
Sender: news@decuac.dec.com (USENET News System)
Nntp-Posting-Host: hussar.dco.dec.com
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation, Washington ULTRIX Resource Center
References: <1992Sep18.181137.134@panix.com> <1992Sep19.234610.26621@decuac.dec.com> <1992Sep21.151623.28853@panix.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 1992 23:54:03 GMT
Lines: 116

ss@panix.com (Steve Steinberg) writes:
Discussion related to the topic:
>>>Has anyone compared SCSI and DSSI drives?  DSSI is a lot more expensive,
>>>is there a difference in seek or transfer rates??

I'll re-order some of the answers for convenience.
>>1) "Are you sure you're going to be I/O bound?" is the first question.
>Yes
>>3) "Is the bulk of your I/O reads?"
>Yes
>>4) "Does your I/O fall in cache-friendly patterns?"
>?

	The reason for this question was because if you're mostly
doing reads, you can gain more "across the board" by adding ram and
increasing the disk cache size *if* there is a cache friendly access
pattern. By "cache friendly" I mean one where you may access the
same block of data repeatedly. Typical UNIX systems fulfil something
like 80-90% of all reads from cache. That means that only some
small percentage of all reads performed actually require that the
disk get involved at all.
	If you increase the cache size AND your data is prone to
caching well, then you speed up ALL I/O on the system and it's a
lot cheaper than expensive disks. However, if all you do is linearly
read large datasets from one end to the other, caching has very
minimal effect, and it's not going to help you.
	I generally (wherever possible that is!) prefer to buy RAM
than more expensive disks. Disks are finicky and only impact a small
subset of the system performance. More RAM helps virtual memory, and
can help disk thruput and other stuff.

	Another possible answer is to examine disk striping sets,
and to take advantage of track caching and head seek minimizing.
I'll defer this one for Allan Rollow. ;)
	

>>2) "What O/S are you using?"
>Ultrix 4.2

	This was just a mandatory sanity check - I'm not qualified to
say *anything* about VMS.

	Note that Ultrix4.3 has several new features buried down in
the filesystem that make it much faster than the Ultrix4.2 filesystem.
You might want to give 4.3 a shot.

>>5) "Have you considered IPI?" if #1 above is true.
>What is IPI?

	IPI disks are pretty expensive but have very impressive
thruput. Higher than DSSI, in fact. Genroco, Inc. makes a turbo
channel IPI controller that's bloody quick. Note that the numbers
listed below are for spiral read performance and spiral writes, so
real life numbers will actually be slower, but still WAY above
SCSI:
SEAGATE
  Elite's are 5.25". The Sabre's 8".
ST41201K        Elite-1         1.20 GB         3.00 MB/sec
ST82500K        Sabre-6         2.50 GB         3.00 MB/sec
ST83220K        Sabre-7         3.22 GB         4.67 MB/sec
ST81154K        Sabre-5 2HP     1.15 GB         6.00 MB/sec
ST83050K        Sabre-7 2HP     3.05 GB         9.34 MB/sec

>>6) "Have you tried more RAM and increasing cache?" if #4 and #3 are true
>The system is not yet in production.  I can answer some of the above
>questions since I know how the system is performing on it's current HW.
>BTW we do have Prestoserve.

	Presto will help write performance only. It especially
helps NFS or DBMS' that do synchronous mode writes, but since
certain filesystem metadata is written synchronously, you get
about a 15% *write* speed increase through the filesystem with
presto. It helps reads some teeny weeny negligible amount.

>The way that I read your response I guess your saying that DSSI is
>much faster than SCSI, but you are not sure if the price difference
>would justify the speed difference, especially if performance is 
>acceptable or can be improved by other means.

	Yes. I wouldn't even say that DSSI is "much faster" than
SCSI. It's a little bit faster than SCSI. The reason I posed all
these questions to you was because I wondered if you were already
at the performance wall, or if you could save some significant
bux and use SCSI instead.

>My problem is that I'm gun-shy.  I'm converting the application from a
>platform with very poor response time and can't afford to make that
>mistake again.  

	Pre-optimizing is a bad idea in computing in general.

	If your code is I/O bound, is there anything you can do to
approach your I/O differently and speed it up by improving your
algorithms?

>The additional cost for DSSI could be warrented so that we can all have a
>warm cozy feeling the day that the system goes live.  

	I work for Digital so we'll all have warm fuzzies if you
buy DSSI drives. ;) Seriously, though, I'd ask your sales rep for
the drive specs first. I think you'll find that seek time and
latencies are very close. And that's what matters most. IPI is
quicker than both for a fair margin.
	I've seen AIM benchmarks done on a 5500 with both SCSI
and DSSI disks. One interesting thing is that the system with DSSI
disks was a *HAIR* slower in response time for a lower user load,
and a *HAIR* faster for a higher user load. To me this would make
me wonder how your application performs, etc, etc. If I were you
I'd take a long hard look at how your application does I/O
before I bought any disks at all.

>My big question is: How much faster is DSSI over SCSI???

	Some. Not a WHOLE lot.

mjr.


