re: talk about performance dropping when clustered:


If your performance is dropping that much by clustering, then you may
have other problems in your area than just the CPU speed.  Is your disk
server sufficient enough to keep up with the demand?  

Another thing to try is to copy many of the 'most used' applications and
system executable images to the local disk and redefine sys$sysroot to
point to that directory FIRST, then on to the others as it originally did.
This will cause the system to look to the local disk first before moving
to the network to get the image.

Also, turn highwater marking off on the internal disk if all it is doing is
paging and swapping and serving images.  Be sure the pagefile is contiguous 
as well.  

To properly build a page/swap disk first init the disk with the index in
either the beginning (better) or end or the disk.  Turn off highwater marking
and then create the pagefile /contiguous.  You can then optionally turn high-
water marking back on.

..and then buy another 8Mb of memory to the full 24, with the full compliment
of memory, that machine is MUCH better, really.  You may also consider getting
an RZ24l to put inside instead of the RZ23l.

						.dave.

