[ A bunch of people were writing commentary on STORME and SCSI vs. DSSI... ]
.
>
>Well, we've already gotten such a nondisclosure (last week), but it left us
>with a lot of questions.  In particular, there seems to be some fuzz around
>what types of drives.  I can't say what DEC told us (they don't call it
>"nondisclosure" for nothing), but there was more on when the products would
>be available than details of the actual hardware.  I already knew that DSSI
>would be supported as an interconnect, but what about the drives themselves?
>If DSSI isn't much better than SCSI, then I can see why they might settle on
>SCSI only.  And we knew that SDI drives were a dying breed from product
>offerings.

I'm not sure what can be said about the product since no official
annoucements have been made.  I will say that for the boxes I've
personally seen the only things that could be plugged into the
boxes were SCSI.
>
>Also, what are the differences between the DSSI and SCSI buses?

The following is the list of DSSI bus features taken from the
"Digital Storage Technology Handbook", public version.  I don't
know what the corresponding SCSI features are.

o  Single 8 bit parallel multidrop data path with both byte parity
   and packet EDC.

o  Peak bandwidth of 4 Mbytes/sec (> 3.75 MB/sec usable).

o  Standard length of 6 meters; other tested configurations using
   longer cables are available (1).

o  Up to eight nodes (storage elements plus host adapters) (2).

o  Seven control lines (16 lines total).

o  CMOS transceivers (DC coupled).

o  Low bus and node interface overhead.

o  Distributed-fair round robin arbitrarion or fixed-priority
   arbitration.

o  Interconnect characteristic impedance 65-86 ohms.

o  Terminator equivalent impedance 84.4 ohms.

The two points worth commentary are #1, cable length and #2, eight nodes.

1.  I believe this is a strong guarantee than made by single ended
    SCSI.  In some SCSI configurations, bus lengths less than 6
    meters may still have problems.  This may be due to vendors
    that take more liberties with the spec then they should.  With
    DSSI stuff we're about the only vendor and our configurations
    tested to make sure they always work at a particular length.

2.  DSSI supports multiple hosts.  SCSI seems to have the capability
    of it, but I've not heard much it how well it works.

>I've heard
>that they're similar, but what are the practical performance differences?

I think the most performance differences betwene DSSI and SCSI come
from the capabilities of the devices themselves.  I think all DSSI
disks support the equivalent of SCSI command queueing.  With an I/O
queue on the target, it can lots of it's own I/O optimizations.

>Do they both scale up linearly as drives are added?

Yes.  The numbers I've seen suggest that DSSI scales better.  This
numbers were from about two years ago though.  Lots of things have
changed in two years.

>Since the physical
>drives are the same, why does the DSSI controller on the drive cost more?

Because we change more.  There probably some small incremental cost
associated with the improvements of DSSI over SCSI, but the biggest
difference in price comes down to that we change more.  There's lots
of people inside DEC that know we charge too much more, but the people
that set the prices haven't been convinced.  Let your sales person
know and they can pass your options back up the food chain.

