In article <1992Sep21.235403.24693@decuac.dec.com>, mjr@hussar.dco.dec.com (Marcus J. "Buddy can you spare a clue?" Ranum) writes:
>
> [ A reply to Steve Steinberg's reply in which the two try work the
>   I/O characteristics of Steve's application and my name is taken
>   in vain. ]

>ss@panix.com (Steve Steinberg) writes:
>Discussion related to the topic:
>>>>Has anyone compared SCSI and DSSI drives?  DSSI is a lot more expensive,
>>>>is there a difference in seek or transfer rates??

I'll get to this one later by providing an example.

>
>	Another possible answer is to examine disk striping sets,
>and to take advantage of track caching and head seek minimizing.
>I'll defer this one for Allan Rollow. ;)
>
(Alan actually), but enough for nits.  In an application where
you have I/O on multiple disks and the load isn't easily
balanced across the disks Striping can be used to help with
the load balancing.  For SCSI disks without command queueing,
it works best if you can spread the disks out over multiple
busses.  On the DECsystem 5500 this can only be done by
putting some disks on the internal SCSI adapter and some on
a KZQSA.

The DSSI disks on the other hand do have command queueing, so
you may be able to get more I/O going through the DSSI adapter
than the SCSI adapter.

For the real performance differences in the DSSI and SCSI
disks you have to look at the I/O loads.  It's likely the
drives will have comparable media transfer speeds and
similar cache sizes.  If these are much the same, the read
performance of the two should be nearly the same.  But if
you follow Marcus' suggestions about using lots of cache
for file system I/O, read performance may not matter much.

For file system writes (or asynchronous raw writes), the
lack of command queueing (or lack of support for it) may
hurt the performance of the SCSI drives.  Given the oppurtunity
ULTRIX will start as many writes as the DSSI drives are willing
to accept.  For either delayed or non-delayed writes if you're
write intensive will manage to keep a disk fairly busy.  With
Striping it can keep a whole set of disks as busy as the bandwidth
of the bus will allow.  For synchronous writes you need lots of
processes writing to take advantage of this.

It's worth noting before ULTRIX V4.3 command queueing in drives
doesn't help reads much because there is so little read-ahead.
The I/O enhancement features in V4.3 probably change this.

>
>>>5) "Have you considered IPI?" if #1 above is true

It's worth noting that Genroco has gotten even more impressive
I/O performance with their drives, when using them with Striping.

>
>> [ More commenary on performance differences in DSSI and SCSI. ]

Here's an example of two very similar disks; one DSSI and the other
SCSI.  Consider the RZ57 and RF72.

			RZ57		RF72
Capacity:		1 GB		1 GB
Media Transfer rate:	2.2 MB/sec	2.0 MB/sec
Average seek time:	14.5 ms		13.4 ms
Cache size:		4 requests	4 tracks
			256 KB		128 KB
average access time:    22.8 ms		21.7 ms

The RZ57 has a better media transfer speed, but the RF72 has a
better seek time and access time.  The larger cache on the RZ57
probably helps read performance.  If you compare other similar
drives, you'll probably find the performance very much the same.

DSSI has some performance advantages over SCSI right now, but I'm
not sure if they are worth the excessive price.  On the other hand,
I've got an application that I/O bound and it's a severe problem,
then the extra money may be worth it.  Personally, I'd look at
getting some of both and spreading the I/O load around as much
as possible.

Striping may help with load balancing, but the current driver insists
that the member devices have the same number of sectors per track.  I'm
not sure if this is useful restriction and wouldn't take long to fix.  Unfortunately for me to fix requires a month or of testing, building
a new version of everything and so on.  A couple of hours of consulting
time from your local Software Services person, could do it much more
cheaply (and I'd still probably end up doing it...)

>
>>My big question is: How much faster is DSSI over SCSI???
>
>	Some. Not a WHOLE lot.

Agreed.

