Hello,

We are currently using a multi-file Rdb database that is split across
multiple disks in a VAXcluster environment.  (current version of Rdb: 4.0,
VMS is V5.5) Much work has been put into tuning the database, but we are
limited by not being able to modify the code of the primary application
that makes use of the database.  At this point the problems that we face
boil down to locking issues which are irritated by business practices.
While we can work to educate our user community on the best ways to use
the current technology, I am interested in investigating alternative
technologies.  The hardware and VMS sides are fairly well tweaked so that
leads to an investigation of the database engine (we cannot dump the
application).

I have heard some very interesting reports about Borland's Interbase
engine, and since it was designed and built by one of Digital's DSRI
architects and Rdb implementors, I have a good feeling about the
feasibility of swapping out Rdb in favor of Interbase.  My main interest
at this point is not in development, but salvaging an existing application
and Interbase's entirely different approach to locking looks quite
interesting. 

So, the questions and a call for opinions or experiences:  (I will
summarize any mail that I receive to comp.databases)

Interbase is fully DSRI compliant so any code linked against Rdb will
continue to run.  Although I have heard that Digital is moving away from
the DSRI interface, I expect that there is enough code out there using
this interface that it will be tough for them to drop support for DSRI any
time soon though.  Opinions?

It would seem that with Interbase's multi-generational approach to storing
data, the physical size of the database would either be more volatile or
need to be sized to accommodate the extra generations.  The life of a
generation seems to be determined by transaction duration and frequency of
garbage collection.  I have heard of troubles with the garbage collection
routines on UNIX, but have no data on the VMS implementation.  Opinions?

VMS supplies a robust lock manager, Interbase runs on many platforms
including VMS; is the VMS lock manager used in the VMS implementation?  If
so, it would seem to be a clean, low impact method of a locking service
when on VMS and extend neatly from either stand-alone to clustered
environments.  It would also seem that the Interbase implementation would
be more complex given a VMS specific locking service and their own service
for other platforms that may not provide a locking service in the OS. 
Opinions?

From the information that I have so far, the database must be contained
within a single disk on VMS.  Is this correct?  We currently use large
RAID disk subsystems so the spindle/performance issue is handled, but we
also have solid state disk which might be nice to utilize for certain
portions of the database.  Anybody doing things like this?  Can a relation
span storage areas?

My current understanding is that hashed indices are not supported, can
anyone refute this?

In terms of the actual conversion, it looks like the best approach would
be to use RMU to export the Rdb database, install Interbase, play a few
games with logicals so that the Interbase images get used, and then use
RMU to import the database.  Before doing this I would expect to have to
structure the database so that there are no techniques used that are not
currently supported in Interbase (hashed indices per the above question,
any others?).  This would get me a complete copy of the database that is
usable by all of our existing code.  Sounds too good to be true, is it?

I would be most interested in answers to the above questions, as well as
any experiences that folks have had with Interbase on VMS and migrations
from Rdb to Interbase.  If there are other concerns that I have not
brought up, I would be interested in those as well.  Many thanks!

--Larry
