On the contrary, I purposefully did not mention these things.  I have been too
jaded by a capitalistic economy to expect prices to fall forever.  However, 
the ability of a piece of equipment or software to accomplish what is needed/
wanted of it, that is most definitely in the interest of a good capitalist.

>	Another problem is cost. You describe several desirable attributes
>with respect to ease of use and interoperability, but such things do not
>come cheap.  /* things deleted about Ranum's axiom ... */

Fine, what is wrong with paying for what you want?  These things are not
that expensive today, if you know what you are doing and where to look.
I pity the people who do not know what they are doing or where to look.  Those
are the ones that do lose in the long run, and perhaps companies will 
completely cater to those folks.

Until then, it seems to me that "Open systems", as I described before, is
the closest thing that we have now.  Of course price is a consideration for
any computer purchase, but that should not be the *first* thing people look at.
It's easy to put forth an economy model that says something like:

Company A has a computer system for $XXXX.
Company B has a computer system that will work with Company A's system for 
$XXXXXXX.  Therefore, eventually Company B will go out of business and then
Company A will take over and make computing closed again.

This is not a good model of the computing industry.  The first and foremost
problem with it is that there are no longer just Company A's and B's, I'd dare
say it would go into the Company ZZZZ's.  For the sake of arguement how about
just listing the main giants.  IBM, DEC, SUN, HP, SGI, Intel(Yes they do make
computer systems), Motorola, NeXT.  Some of these are bigger than the others
granted.  But, they all have their hot little hands into the UNIX user 
community.  Is this because they all think that UNIX is the way to go?  
No, it is because that is what the market has demanded of them, and they are
all leading to "Open Systems".  Oh yes, I forgot AT&T in there... probably more
than that, but this longwinded post has gone long enough. 


P.S. I am only speaking of the UNIX community mainly because I have seen more
     progress for other platforms that include pathways from their (apple, ibm,
     dec,  sun, sgi, hp, etc..) machines to the unix environment.



