anthony@yoyo.cc.monash.edu.au (Anthony Baxter) writes:
> Does anyone have any other helpful suggestions for improving system
> performance?

> options		AUDIT
> options		SYS_TRACE
> pseudo-device	audit
> pseudo-device	sys_trace

You might think about eliminating these and turning off any sort of
auditing.  At least turn off acct by "accton" and comment out the entry in
/etc/rc.

Another performance gain can be had by editing /usr/sys/conf/mips/param.c
and enabling cache_bufcache:

> int     cache_bufcache = 1;

You're probably looking for nifty kernel hacks but I don't know as much as I
should about them.

Here are some non-kernel stuff which you've probably looked at already:

Are you running routed?  If so, kill it, disable from /etc/rc.local and use
static routing (see route(8c)).

Does the machine receive email from the outside world?  If it doesn't, kill
the sendmail process and disable from /etc/rc.local.  You can still send
email out - you just can't receive.

Did you hash your password file?  If not, then "mkpasswd /etc/passwd".

Did you freeze your sendmail.cf file?  If not, then "/usr/lib/sendmail -bz".

If you're not exporting any NFS directories, disable nfsd from /etc/rc.local.
If you're not mounting any NFS directories, disable biod from /etc/rc.local.

If you're not doing any NFS, disable NFS from the kernel.

Are there useless entries in /etc/crontab, or /var/spool/at?

If lots of people are running emacs at the same time, then you might think
about the draconian method of asking them use jove or something that takes
up less memory.

What other services do you provide on your system, or were you just
interested in kernel mods?

