Organization: Kernel Mode Consulting, San Diego, CA
Lines: 76

(cross-posted, so to speak, to DECUServe.  This is a slightly later version 
with a few typos fixed.)

Is there any hope at all of convincing Digital to get rid of the "OpenVMS"
moniker?

I talk to lots of folks in "the field".  

	Many think that it makes DEC look silly.  

	Most simply dismiss it as a marketing smokescreen.   

        Some think that it will actually backfire -- if there's a marketing
        smokescreen, there must be something behind it that DEC wants to hide.

None of them thinks that DEC is going to sell any more VMS licenses just
because DEC tacked "Open" onto the front of the name.

Personally I wish that DEC would "demonstrate its commitment to open systems"
by MEANINGFUL efforts.  Such as:

	Continuing to make internals documentation, specifically including the
	IDSM and the VMS listings kits, available on for all VMS platforms on
	a timely basis, while increasing the scope of these offerings.  

	Continuing to support hardware standards, such as SCSI for mass
	storage and SIMM slots for memory, that allow customers to buy 
	components for DEC systems in the aggressively-priced PC marketplace.
	There is good progess here in the low end, but at the high end, 
	DEC is still trying to sell disk storage for many times the market
	price just because the drives have the all-hallowed SDI connector on
	them.  Why not build an HSC that can handle SCSI devices?  (I know --
	it's called a VAXstation!)  Why not provide memory cards for the 
	high-end VAXes with SIMM slots on them?  
	Customers should not have to support DEC's mass storage or memory 
	divisions if they feel they can get a better deal elsewhere.  If 
	such groups within DEC can't compete on the, er, 'open' market,
	they deserve to lose.  Real competition will make for a healthier DEC.

	Providing REASONABLY PRICED upgrade paths and entry points for new 
	technology -- especially for software developers.  Things aren't
	bad right now in the VAX area but Alpha is going to require a *huge*
	buy-in.  I'll say more about this in another note. 

	Being a little bit quicker to adopt good ideas from other operating
	systems.  VMS users have been asking for command-line I/O redirection
	(e.g. Unix <, >, and | operators), setuid-like functionality (on VMS
	this equates to install-with-identifier), and other good Unix ideas
	for OVER A DECADE.  

Digital needs to realize the reasons that it lost sales to Unix-based systems:
For a long time DEC refused to compete on a level price playing field; DEC
refused to add to VMS a few Unix-like features that customers (especially developers)
dearly wanted; and DEC refused to take the workstation market seriously, 
offering slow machines at high prices.  

"Open systems" looked like the answer to a lot of folks who got burned by DEC
in the past.  The promise was that with a Unix platform you wouldn't be locked
into a single vendor who wouldn't sell you what you want at a competitive
price.  But while "open systems" was assumed to be the answer, "closed systems"
were not the problem!!!  The problem was "can't get what we want from DEC"!  No
one would have minded being "locked in" to DEC and VMS had VAXes and their
peripherals and software been priced competitively with the rest of the market,
and if DEC had made available the desired software features! 

VMS and Alpha will succeed to the extent that DEC fixes its problems of the
past.  Whether or not VMS has "Open" in front of its name won't affect the
outcome in the slightest. But "OpenVMS" is a clumsy name that smacks of
marketing hype, something we could all use a little less of.  Let's go back to
"VMS".  Please? 

