morse@quark.mpr.ca (Daryl Morse) writes:


:In article <1992Sep07.180426.11417@cadlab.sublink.org> martelli@cadlab.sublink.org (Alex Martelli) writes:
:
:   lsgriffith@gems.vcu.edu writes:
:	   ...
:>   :I think it would be a very strong and positive strategic statement for
:>   :DEC to have one box capable of running all THREE operating systems!
:
:>   I think it spells confusion.
:
:Yah, the market is pretty confused, wanting DOS, Windows, OS/2,
:Windows/NT, UNIX, ULTRIX, OSF/1, DG-UX, HP-UX, SunOS, Solaris, SCO,
:VMS, RSTS, VM, MVS, MPE, etc., etc., etc. DEC should force the entire
:market to use one operating system by only supporting <take your pick>
:on the Alpha systems. 8-) Try and tell me that wouldn't go over like a
:lead balloon.

But DEC is ***NOT*** supporting Ultrix, Dg-Ux, Hp-Ux, SunOS (which is
the same thing as Solaris 1.x), RSTS, VM, MVS nor MPE on the Alpha
platform (and a good thing, too); Ultrix users, for example, although
coming from DEC-sold platforms, will have to port and migrate if they
want to use Alpha platforms.  Thus, according to your silly argument
"DEC is forcing the entire market to use only three operating systems" --
of course no such things applies, very simply if somebody is irrevocably
committed to, say, MVS, then he or she is going to have to keep buying
IBM mainframes or clones thereof, rather than switching to Alpha boxes.

Or do you think "a very strong and positive strategic statement" would
be made by DEC compounding the confusion and announcing another dozen
operating systems for said Alpha boxes?

A *strategic direction* should be based on ONE main operating system for
a box, possibly with niche offerings for particular exigencies (much
the way Unix variants were offered on VAX boxes, but VMS was the main,
STRATEGIC diretion).  Offering 3, or 15, incompatible operating systems,
on the same box, *SPELLS CONFUSION* in strategic direction of the firm;
the classic example being IBM's muddle betweeen VM, MVS, and VSE, on 370.

:>   :What other workstation would be able to boast such flexibility?
:
:>   Any workstation based on Intel-386 and similar chips.  Far more than three
:>   operating systems are available and reasonably popular on such boxes.
:
:Sure, you can count far more than three if you include DOS, OS/2 *,
:Windows *, and UNIX * as separate operating systems. I wouldn't be
:quite so generous!

Surely even you must understand that "UNIX *" ARE several different
operating systems?  Generosity has nothing to do with it.  Ultrix and
OSF/1 are two widely different OS's that do (or will) run on the same
DEC-built MIPS-based boxes, just as Ultrix and System V and BSD4.3Tahoe
and VMS are four widely different OS's that do (or did) run on the same
DEC-built VAX-based boxes, just as MSDOS, DRDOS, OS/2, SCO Xenix, SCO
Unix, AT&T Unix SVR3.2, AT&T Unix SVR4, MW Coherent, BSDI386, Mt Xinu's
MACH386, Solaris 2.0, NextStep and Linux are 13 (just for luck) widely
different OS's that do, did, and/or will run on the same Intel-based
boxes (whoever built the box).

Note that the proliferation of OS's on Intel-based boxes is obviously
dependent on the LACK of strategy behind them -- Intel, as a chip
house, will be just to glad to sell its silicon for any OS, or other
purpose, whatsoever; Intel-based boxes have thus become a commodity.
Suppliers of SYSTEMS, today, tend to have clearer strategic goals,
although of course DEC is not the only confused puppy around; IBM's
goals, if any, are just as murky -- note how the profit and loss
statements of DEC and IBM are going, and compare with those of the
other big two workstation suppliers who DO have clear strategic
directions in the OS field, Sun and HP.

:quite so generous!  You may not think it's of value, but the ability
:to run VMS on an Intel x86 or Px is something that will, IMHO, never
:see the light of day. So given that Alpha supports Windows/NT, OSF/1,
:and VMS, it has a leg up on Intel in that respect, IMHO.

But similarly, and just for example:  OS/2 just as likely will never be
ported to Alpha, so Intel-based boxes have a leg up on Alpha-based ones
in that respect.  And there are so many more niche OS's on Intel-based
boxes, that they'll have so many legs up that they'll look like so many
centipedes.  So what?  Alpha-based boxes will still be preferable IF
their speed, quality and cost (including excellent support for ONE OS
satisfying a wide market segment) are such as to dominate those of boxes
based on Intel chips.  DEC's fragmenting the market of Alpha based boxes
into three incompatible segments from the very beginning looks to me
like a very bad way to start meeting these ambitious objectives.  Just
for starters, us independent suppliers of application software will have
to decide which one or more of the several Alpha-based environments to
support; assuming the 3 OS's meet with equal success, the potential
market per port has thus been cleverly divided by three.
