In article <1992Sep12.184759.17019@ultb.isc.rit.edu>, whd0675@ritvax.isc.rit.edu writes:
> I have a question for you hardware experts out there.  What monitors are
> compatable with the VCB02 subsystem?  The monitors can either be DEC or 
> non-DEC.  Actually, I would be quite interested in knowing if there are
> any third-party monitors that would work with the VCB02.  I am aware that
> there is a VR260, VR262, and VR290 from DEC.  Are there any others?
> Thanks in advance!

The relatively new NEC Multisync 5FG (17", street price $1400) and 6FG (20",
street price $2400) will work with *all four* of DEC's workstation video
standards: 60 Hz 1024x864 (GPX, which is what you're using), 66 Hz and 72 Hz
1280x1024 (SPX), and the 1024x768 that comes out of low-end VS 4000/VLCs (which
will drive a stock Sony 1304, btw).  They have BNC RGB inputs - just hook up
and go. Spearate H and H/V sync inputs are also provided; the monitor takes
sync wherever it finds it (ie DEC RGB uses sync on green, while others have one
or two separate sync lines). 

I'll bet they'd work with Suns also, but I haven't tried this. 

In addition to the BNC inputs, they also have both standard and mini Dsub-15
connectors and will work with just about every PC and Mac video standard ever
invented.  There is a front-panel switch to select between the BNC and D-sub
connectors so you can use the monitor on two machines at once without external
video switching boxes.  The two video sources do not have to use the same
timing. 

The NEC tubes have a polished glass face rather than an anti-reflection
coating. You can buy an add-on anti-reflection lens if you need one.  This may
seem like a kludgy approach, but any AR coating makes the image a little less
bright and a little less sharp; this way, if you don't need an AR screen, you
don't have to look through one.  And you can clean the tube face with ordinary
glass cleaner. 

In case you hadn't guessed I'm very impressed with these.  When I compared them
side-by-side I thought that the 6FG provided a sharper, crisper image than
DEC's VRT19 (19" Trinitron),  which lists for about $4000, and only works with
some DEC workstations.  

