I'm surprised no one has mentioned the first law of open systems: that
no vendor expects their open systems to be connected to any one else's
open systems. Look at the NIS/DNS fiasco:

	Sun insist that if you want to use DNS you have to use NIS
	(people have posted workarounds to this).

	IBM insist (AIX3.1.5) that if you want to run NIS there are
	some databases (services and protocols I think), which have
	to be on NIS. Document it? Are you crazy?? You'll be wanting
	technical support next!

	Likewise Convex, but at least they document it. They then set
	up mail to use totally different routing methods depending on
	whether you are running DNS standalone or via NFS. Do they
	document this? That's like asking if they employ enough people
	in techical support.

Open Systems and Unix seem to be totally foreign languages to IBM. Sun
and Convex are moving from the you're-in-control-and-can-choose-what-
you-do-and-don't-want-to-install approach to the IBM you-either-configure-
everything-just-like-we-do-or-you're-on-your-own philosophy.

And I haven't even mentioned Sun's attitude to X. However the *worst*
vendor of the three above must be IBM. 

John Rowe
Systems Manager
Theoretical Physics
Exeter University
UK


