The DECpc 333sx LP: Windows Power at the Entry Level

HIGHLIGHTS

o  The fastest 386SX processor on the market today

o  A PC price/performance winner in a great package

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

The lowest priced model in the family of new "Built Better. Priced Right." 
personal computers designed, engineered and manufactured by Digital is the 
DECpc 333sx LP.  The DECpc 333sx LP gives customers the power and 
reliability they need at a truly competitive price.

The most popular desktop systems on the market today are the 386SX systems. 
They have all the power needed to run advanced business applications, such 
as those that run under Microsoft Windows.  This product runs at 33 MHz. 
Most other vendors offer 386SX systems running at 25 MHz or even slower 
speeds.

Additional features of the DECpc 333sx LP (beyond the family features): 

o  Uses a 386SX microprocessor running at 33 MHz.

o  On-board video controller, Super VGA (SVGA), supports resolutions up to 
   1024-by-768 (video has 512 KB of memory.  The controller does not use a 
   bus slot).  Video memory is expandable up to 1 MB for "true color" 
   applications.   The standard system supports up to 72 Hz monitors, which 
   reduces eye strain and discomfort.  Most competitive systems support only 
   60 Hz monitors.

o  Packaged configurations are available in 2 MB or 4 MB, expandable to 14 
   MB on the motherboard.  Memory is 70ns, faster than the 80ns memory often 
   used by other vendors.

o  64 KB Cache for better performance.  Competitive 386SX models usually 
   have no cache at all.

o  Optional -- the fastest 387 math coprocessor for additional performance.

o  Aggressively priced, starting at $899 for a fully configured 2 MB system, 
   (including MS-DOS V5.0 and Windows V3.1 installed) plus $350 for a 
   14-inch color monitor.



The Upgradable Systems: Power with the Future in Mind



PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

For high performance and fast graphics in business-critical applications, 
the new upgradable DECpc LP family is the answer.  These systems are based 
on 386DX and 486 technology and include many features right on the 
motherboard, such as the SVGA video running on a fast local bus.  A modular 
daughter card supports a variety of 386DX and 486 processors for upgrading 
as needs change or grow.  All this, yet the upgradable systems are very 
competitively priced.

Additional features of the upgradable systems (beyond the family features):

o  Upgradable from 386DX and 486SX through 486DX to 486DX2.

   The DECpc 340dx LP has a dedicated socket for installation of the fastest 
   math coprocessor available.  This is an easy and economical way of 
   speeding floating point calculations, enhancing the performance of 
   spreadsheets, CAD, and other math-intensive applications.

   The DECpc 425sx LP, 433dx LP, 450d2 LP, and 466d2 LP all have a dedicated 
   Vacancy Socket that provides an industry standard path for processor 
   upgrades.  For example, a 486SX can be upgraded to a 486DX or 486DX2; a 
   486DX can be upgraded to a 486DX2.

   
   All models have a modular, snap-in daughter card for replacement of 
   microprocessor and cache, allowing the entire system to be upgraded with 
   more powerful processors and cache.  A Daughter Card Upgrade can convert 
   a 340DX to a 466DX2 (or anything in between) in one step by Digital 
   Services.

o  Memory -- fast and lots of it.

   Unlike many competitors that require additional memory to be added on a 
   memory card inserted into an ISA slot or onto some other optional board, 
   the entire LP family has room for memory expansion right on the 
   motherboard.  There is room for up to 64 MB of system RAM (4 MB is 
   standard).

   The memory is 70 nanosecond, unlike that of many competitors that use 
   ordinary, slower 80 nanosecond memory.  The 70ns memory can substantially 
   increase the performance of memory-intensive applications.

o  Cache makes the upgradables even better performing.

   These systems include the most effective caching strategy, 128 KB of 
   Writeback Cache, which significantly boosts performance.  The 
   cache is optionally expandable to 256 KB.


o  Ultra fast local bus SVGA video performance with GUI acceleration.

   The DECpc SVGA video has been optimized to run graphical applications 
   like Microsoft Windows up to four times faster than before.  Windows 
   screens will appear to "snap open," and switching from one application to 
   another will seem instantaneous.  This is accomplished by the following 
   features:

   -  Local Bus SVGA Video -- The video controller runs faster because a 
      dedicated local bus directly connects the video to the processor.  
      This means that information travels on a fast, 32-bit data path 
      running at the speed of the system clock (currently up to 33 MHz), 
      instead of the standard 16-bit data path running at the 8 MHz speed of 
      the ISA bus, which is where most video is run on other vendors' PCs.

      Another advantage of local bus video is that the ISA bus is freed from 
      carrying video information so that peripherals and options already on 
      the ISA bus access the ISA bus faster.  The net result is that the 
      whole system gains performance.

   -  Graphical User Interface (GUI) Accelerator -- The dedicated video 
      accelerator chip on the motherboard is optimized to perform the 
      graphics functions identified by Microsoft as critical for Windows.  
      This means that the GUI, with its windows and pull-down menus, runs 
      substantially faster.  The result is that functions like scrolling, 
      which can take up to 15 seconds per page with ordinary SVGA, is 
      processed in one or two seconds.

      Another advantage is that the system processor (the 486 chip) does not 
      need to calculate the process-intensive GUI information.  Therefore, 
      it is freed to perform other calculations at the same time that the 
      video chip performs video calculations.  The result is that the whole 
      system performs even faster still.

   -  High Resolution -- The systems come with 512 KB of video VRAM, more 
      than many competitors.  Our standard configuration is capable of 
      resolutions of up to 1024-by-768.  Or, at lower resolutions, the 
      system is capable of reproducing hundreds of colors.  With the 
      optional video VRAM, the DECpc is capable of both 1024-by-768 high 
      resolution and 256 colors.  VRAM is used instead of DRAM for 
      additional performance.


