UNIX VS. WINDOWS NT Technical issues that are a toss-up between Unix and Windows
NT

o Advanced hierarchical file system with long descriptive names

o 32-bit linear memory model

o Preemptive multitasking

o Memory-mapped files

o Shared libraries

o Integral peer networking

o Distributed global naming (assuming LAN Manager NT)

Areas in which NT looks better than Unix

o Threaded SMP (symmetric multiprocessing). Some flavors of Unix (e.g.,
NextStep) are threaded but lack SMP, while others (e.g., SVR4) are SMP-capable
but not threaded. Solaris 2.0 claims threaded SMP.

o Layered device-driver architecture. NT will provide generic disk, display,
network, and printer drivers that will allow driver writers to specialize
relatively easily to create specific device drivers. Unix drivers are more
monolithic and, hence, more difficult to create and maintain.

o Interapplication communication. NT provides well-defined services (e.g., OLE,
or Object Linking and Embedding); Unix does not.

o A well-integrated scalable-font subsystem. While SVR4.2 will offer Adobe Type
Manager and NextStep has Display PostScript, the dominant Unix GUI, X Window
System, still relies heavily on bit-mapped fonts.

o DOS/1 6-bit Windows support (unproven, but likely to equal or exceed that of
OS/2 2.0 and far exceed that of any Unix).

o Source code-compatible applications, with a hugely successful Windows 3.x
installed base.

Areas in which Unix looks better than NT

o Multiuser capabilities.

o Distributable GUI (X)Qgraphical applications are intrinsically client/server.

o Well-standardized basic network services (TCP/IP, sockets, RPC [remote
procedure calls]) that work reliably across wildly different hardware platforms
and operating system versions. NT offers a lot of options (named pipes, mail
slots, NetBlOS, sockets, RPC, and even, potentially, DDE/OLE, if and when they
are made network-aware), but the danger is that there may be no clear path for
developers to write distributed NT applications.

o Well-standardized wide-area E-mail.

o Availability for more platforms than are likely, at least in the short term,
for NT.

